# ADR-020: Deployment and Scaling Strategy

| Field | Value |
|-------|-------|
| ADR | 020 |
| Title | Deployment and Scaling Strategy |
| Status | Accepted |
| Date | 2026-07-27 |
| Decision Makers | R-zension Engineering Team |

---

# Context

R-zension is designed as a cloud-native SaaS platform that must support growth from a single development environment to thousands of organizations and millions of users.

The deployment strategy must ensure

- High availability
- Scalability
- Reliability
- Security
- Repeatability
- Low operational complexity

The platform should support incremental infrastructure upgrades without requiring application redesign.

---

# Decision

R-zension will adopt a **Container-Based Cloud-Native Deployment Strategy**.

Applications will be packaged as Docker containers and deployed independently.

Each service may scale horizontally without affecting the rest of the platform.

---

# Deployment Architecture

```text
                    Internet

                        │

                        ▼

               Reverse Proxy / Load Balancer

                        │

        ┌───────────────┼────────────────┐

        ▼               ▼                ▼

   React Client    Express API     Oracle AI

        │               │                │

        └───────────────┼────────────────┘

                        ▼

                     Redis Cache

                        ▼

                  PostgreSQL Database

                        ▼

                Object File Storage
```

---

# Objectives

The deployment architecture should

- Support horizontal scaling
- Minimize downtime
- Enable continuous deployment
- Simplify rollbacks
- Support cloud providers
- Remain infrastructure agnostic

---

# Deployment Environments

Supported environments

- Development
- Testing
- Staging
- Production

Each environment should remain isolated.

---

# Infrastructure Components

Core infrastructure

- Reverse Proxy
- React Client
- Express Backend
- Oracle AI Service
- PostgreSQL
- Redis
- Object Storage

Future infrastructure

- Kubernetes
- CDN
- API Gateway
- Monitoring Stack
- Message Broker

---

# Deployment Flow

```text
Developer

↓

GitHub

↓

CI Pipeline

↓

Automated Tests

↓

Docker Build

↓

Container Registry

↓

Deployment

↓

Health Checks

↓

Production
```

---

# Continuous Integration

Every code change should trigger

- Dependency installation
- Linting
- Unit tests
- Integration tests
- Security scans
- Docker image creation

Deployment should not proceed if quality checks fail.

---

# Continuous Deployment

Deployment stages

```text
Build

↓

Test

↓

Stage

↓

Approval (Optional)

↓

Production
```

Production deployments should be automated wherever practical.

---

# Scaling Strategy

Each service scales independently.

Examples

- React Client
- Express API
- Oracle AI
- Notification Service (Future)

Database scaling follows dedicated database strategies.

---

# Horizontal Scaling

Stateless services should support multiple instances.

Example

```text
          Load Balancer

          │     │     │

          ▼     ▼     ▼

      Backend Backend Backend

          │     │     │

          └─────┼─────┘

                ▼

             PostgreSQL
```

---

# Vertical Scaling

Components supporting vertical scaling

- PostgreSQL
- Redis
- Oracle AI (where appropriate)

Vertical scaling may be combined with horizontal scaling.

---

# Load Balancing

Incoming traffic should be distributed across healthy service instances.

Responsibilities

- Health checking
- Traffic distribution
- SSL termination
- Request forwarding

---

# Health Checks

Every service should expose

```text
GET /health
```

Health checks should verify

- Application readiness
- Database connectivity
- Cache connectivity (if required)
- Critical dependencies

---

# Rollback Strategy

Every deployment should support immediate rollback.

Rollback methods

- Previous container image
- Previous deployment manifest
- Previous configuration

Rollback procedures should be automated where possible.

---

# Configuration Management

Configuration should be supplied through

- Environment variables
- Secret management
- Deployment manifests

Configuration must never be embedded into application binaries.

---

# Secret Management

Store secrets separately from source code.

Examples

- JWT Secret
- Database Password
- API Keys
- AI Provider Credentials
- SMTP Credentials

Secrets should rotate periodically.

---

# Database Deployment

Database schema changes should use versioned migrations.

Migration process

```text
Deploy

↓

Run Migrations

↓

Validate

↓

Application Startup
```

Schema changes should be backward compatible whenever possible.

---

# High Availability

Production deployments should avoid single points of failure.

Critical services

- Backend
- Redis
- PostgreSQL
- Reverse Proxy

Future deployments should support automatic failover.

---

# Disaster Recovery

Recovery planning includes

- Database backups
- Object storage backups
- Infrastructure restoration
- Configuration backups
- Secret recovery

Recovery procedures should be tested regularly.

---

# Monitoring

Track

- CPU usage
- Memory usage
- Network traffic
- Disk usage
- Deployment success
- Container restarts
- Request latency
- Error rates

---

# Logging

All services should write structured logs.

Logs should include

- Timestamp
- Service
- Environment
- Severity
- Request ID
- Correlation ID

Centralized log aggregation is recommended.

---

# Security

Deployment security should include

- HTTPS everywhere
- Firewall rules
- Private internal networking
- Secret management
- Image scanning
- Dependency scanning
- Principle of least privilege

---

# Performance Goals

| Metric | Target |
|---------|---------|
| Deployment Time | < 10 minutes |
| Service Startup | < 60 seconds |
| Rollback Time | < 5 minutes |
| Availability | > 99.9% |

---

# Cloud Compatibility

The deployment architecture should support

- Amazon Web Services (AWS)
- Microsoft Azure
- Google Cloud Platform (GCP)
- DigitalOcean
- Oracle Cloud Infrastructure (OCI)
- Self-hosted infrastructure

The application should remain cloud-provider independent.

---

# Alternatives Considered

## Manual Deployment

Pros

- Simple initially

Cons

- Error-prone
- Difficult rollback
- Poor repeatability
- High operational risk

Decision

Rejected.

---

## Virtual Machine Deployment

Pros

- Familiar infrastructure

Cons

- Slower scaling
- Higher maintenance
- Less efficient resource usage

Decision

Rejected.

---

## Container-Based Deployment

Pros

- Portable
- Repeatable
- Cloud-native
- Easy scaling
- Simplified CI/CD
- Infrastructure independence

Decision

Accepted.

---

# Consequences

Positive

- Independent service scaling
- Faster deployments
- Easier rollbacks
- Improved reliability
- Better resource utilization
- Cloud portability

Negative

- Container orchestration complexity
- Infrastructure monitoring requirements
- Initial DevOps setup effort

---

# Future Evolution

Future deployment enhancements include

- Kubernetes
- Helm Charts
- GitOps
- Blue-Green Deployment
- Canary Releases
- Service Mesh
- Multi-region deployment
- Auto Scaling
- Zero-Downtime Deployments
- Chaos Engineering
- Edge deployments

The current deployment strategy is intentionally designed to support these capabilities with minimal architectural changes.

---

# Decision Summary

R-zension adopts a cloud-native, container-based deployment strategy built around Docker, independent service scaling, automated CI/CD pipelines, and infrastructure portability. This architecture provides a reliable foundation for continuous delivery, high availability, and future large-scale growth.

